          "description": {
            "comment": "Grevy's zebra (Equus grevyi), also known as the imperial zebra, is the largest living wild equid and the most threatened of the three species of zebra, the other two being the plains zebra and the mountain zebra. Named after French president Jules Grevy, it is found in parts of Kenya and Ethiopia (from Wikipedia entry). This sample of individual found killed by a lion was procured by Julien Ayroles and Julie Peng at the Mpala Research Centre in Kenya, and coordinate by them and Daniel Rubenstein of Princeton University to generate a high-quality reference genome for the Vertebrate Genomes Project (VGP). The sample was collected under research license NACOSTI/P/25/4174743. The individual was determined to be female after manual genome curation. Sequencing and genome assembly were conducted at the Vertebrate Genome Lab (VGL) at the Rockefeller University, led by Erich D. Jarvis, Giulio Formenti, and Jennifer Balacco. Funding was supported by William R. Harman and a Google conservation grant to E.D.J.",
